    "created_at": "2020-01-24T20:20:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-01-24T20:22:26Z",
    "body": "@annevk It’s probably also worth factoring out because it’s confusing that type names aren’t guaranteed to be unique. In that algorithm it’s safe to rely on the names because you wouldn’t enter it in the first place unless the type in question were a real typed array type, but it’s still super weird that we can create other types with (e.g.) the type name _Int8Array_:\r\n\r\n1. Escape an interface name:\r\n\r\n```\r\ninterface _Int8Array {};\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. Frozen array shenanigans:\r\n\r\n```\r\ninterface Int8 {};\r\ntypedef FrozenArray<Int8> lol;\r\n```",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
